DEFECT FOUND AND FIXED WHILE MAKING THIS CHANGE
  The degenerate-variance guard in decide() was 'if not se:', an
  exact-zero test. Whether n identical accuracies sum to exactly n*x is a
  floating-point accident that differs by interpreter:
    CPython 3.13: sum([0.5144]*10)/10 == 0.5144 exactly, sample SD 0.0
    CPython 3.11: sum == 5.144000000000001, sample SD 1.1702778228589004e-16
  3.11 is the REGISTERED environment. There the guard did not fire, the
  TOST ran with SE = 3.700743415417188e-17, and decide() returned
  EQUIVALENT on ten identical accuracies - a maximally confident verdict
  from a sample carrying no information. Found because the n = 5 fixture
  happened to produce exact zero and the n = 10 fixture did not.

  Fixed with DEGENERATE_SD_RELATIVE_FLOOR = 1e-12, relative to the
  measurement scale. Each accuracy is a mean of 10^6 Bernoulli trials
  whose own SD near p = 0.5 is about 5e-4, so a real cross-set SD is of
  that order; float noise from summing ten such values is of order 1e-15
  relative. The floor sits three orders above the noise and nine below
  any physically possible value, so it cannot reclassify a real result.
  It is a numerical guard, not a statistical threshold. Both interpreters
  now return INDETERMINATE with degenerate_variance = True.

